{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/586373-help-needed-system-choice","id":586373,"title":"Help needed; System choice","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-07-10T13:01:34-07:00","id":586373,"title":"Help needed; System choice","content":"I'm considering creating a VtR chronicle. I have the troupe lined up already (an old VtM / DtF troupe I've played with and ST'd for before). It would be a nice change of pace for us, yet it has familiar elements <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> Now none of us have played VtR before, and I grabbed a friends 1st edition corebook and my understanding of this setting is coming along nicely. At least in the light of having no practical experience with it. But yesterday I discovered that there's a 2nd edition of the system, which I hear is pretty different.<br /> <br /> So my question is; Which edition is best?<br /> Of course this is a matter of personal oppinion. But as different as they sound, I'm betting you guys can give me an idea of which system best portrays kindred at their most interesting level. And which system generates the most fluent gameplay.<br /> <br /> I thank you for any help and advise.<br /> - MonkeyMann","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3448-monkeymann","name":"MonkeyMann","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3448_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"44"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-10T13:07:19-07:00","id":586403,"title":"","content":"2nd Edition is better IMO, the changes they made to nWoD in the second edition are really well done and the vampires are BRUTAL, and humanity fluctuates in interesting ways. I would suggest doing the city building exercises in the 2nd edtion book as well to help flush out your characters and settings. VtR2ndEdition is just a great and fluid game that allows for a lot of interesting play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5718-hackthulhu","name":"Hackthulhu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5718_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-10T13:13:31-07:00","id":586408,"title":"","content":"I think 2nd edition is much better. Not only mechanically speaking, but also because it has a much more clearly defined identity than 1st edition, especially early 1st edition material. A common complaint against early 1st edition was that it felt like &quot;Masquerade Lite&quot;, in the sense that it borrowed a lot of ideas from Masquerade, but didn't have the same punch. 2nd edition does a much better job of standing on its own two feet as a setting, without trying to define itself by how it is or isn't like Masquerade. Also, this may be anecdotal evidence, but I've known a lot of Masquerade fans who were never able to get into 1st edition Requiem but were won over by 2nd edition.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 07-10-2015, 04:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-10T13:13:40-07:00","id":586409,"title":"","content":"Vampires are generally stronger is the big difference. The game is also set up with a more narrative style, with players being encouraged to take dramatic failures and pursue Aspirations. I personally prefer second edition, but that's because the tone of the first edition never managed to make me realise what vampires are like.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:blue\">Blue is sarcasm.</span><br /> <br /> If I suggestion I make contradicts in-setting metaphysics please ignore me, I probably brought in scientific ideas.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4507-twodsix","name":"TwoDSix","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"833"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-10T13:30:14-07:00","id":586414,"title":"","content":"The new system for 2e alone, with its Conditions, Social Maneuvers, and Beats is novel enough for me to suggest that over 1e. You said you wanted to try Requiem for something different? Then 2e is definitely better if we want to use &quot;different&quot; as the scale, while it still has a number of familiar elements.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-10T13:47:02-07:00","id":586418,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MonkeyMann</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/586373-help-needed-system-choice#post586373\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course this is a matter of personal oppinion. But as different as they sound, I'm betting you guys can give me an idea of which system best portrays kindred at their most interesting level. And which system generates the most fluent gameplay.</div> </div> </div> </div><b>Kindred at their most interesting level:</b><br /> Second edition. VtR was the first of the New World of Darkness monster corebooks, designed and released when the developers were still feeling out the identity of the nWoD, and it shows. Some elements core to VtR have developed over time through supplements and been incorporated into the presentation of the new corebook. A more ground-level game where interpersonal relations with specific living people are more emphasized; a murky shadow history filled with exception cases that prevent single uniform answers to big questions like &quot;why do vampires exist&quot; or &quot;is there a God and, if so, how does He regard us&quot;; a chaotic landscape of domains where each city's political gameboard is unique and covenant positions and demeanors cannot be counted on to be consistent from city to city or night to night. Some of these things are girded by new systems built into the game in second edition. For example, Touchstones incentivize players to develop their supporting cast and give thought to who matters to their character. Others are baked into the corebook's structure. Each vampiric clan has three mutually contradictory &quot;origins&quot; presented as myths told among the Kindred; there is a chapter of sample domains illustrating different possible alignments of the five covenants, and in some cases, covenants unique to certain cities or regions.<br /> <br /> In relation to bringing players in from Vampire: the Masquerade, second edition also does a better job of presenting the ways in which Requiem differs from Masquerade in particular. The first edition corebook reads like it is afraid to venture too far from the nest, carefully measuring innovations against familiar touchstones. In some places, like the sample bloodlines based on Masquerade clans, the result is neither familiar enough to be comfortable nor new enough to be distinct. Second edition is bolder; its text is punchier; it goes further afield.<br /> <br /> <b>The most fluent gameplay:</b><br /> If you mean &quot;fluid&quot; gameplay, moving smoothly and uninterrupted, speaking as a fan of second edition overall, I do have to concede that first edition moves a little more fluidly. Second edition nWoD introduces Conditions, a core mechanic where circumstantial modifiers are identified discretely and come and go in play, and a few important mechanics hook into having or resolving a Condition. I find that Conditions can slow down play just a tad, when a situation calls for one and you think to decide what Condition applies best and what effects it might have. The major suggestions I have for a new group learning Conditions are: a) Write the Condition's name down on an index card and pass it to the player, have the player pass it back when it's resolved; the primary purpose of having a Condition is as a persistent reminder of a thing being around that could be brought into play for a dramatic situation. b) Improvise. Conditions created by specific powers, such as those produced by Majesty and Dominate, should be relatively consistent so that a character with that power has an idea of what he can do in a situation, but most Conditions can be basically treated as freeform. Use common sense. Make up new Conditions if preexisting ones don't fit or just if it's faster than looking at a list; check out the sample effects of Conditions and generalize them as stuff for other Conditions to do in play; treat resolution triggers as suggestions and be open to alternate resolutions. These rules are &quot;softer&quot; than, say, the rules for Discipline effects.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-10T16:14:04-07:00","id":586529,"title":"","content":"Wow, it must have been some re-write. Thank you all for your input. It seems like it will be worth the read to go through the second edition book <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> And Stupid Loserman, thanks for the fluent/fluid correction. English isn't my first language, so I get my words mixed up sometimes :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3448-monkeymann","name":"MonkeyMann","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3448_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"44"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-10T16:26:37-07:00","id":586536,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MonkeyMann</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/586373-help-needed-system-choice?p=586529#post586529\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And Stupid Loserman, thanks for the fluent/fluid correction. English isn't my first language, so I get my words mixed up sometimes :P</div> </div> </div> </div>Naw, I looked it up to be sure and &quot;fluent&quot; does have the same definition in English as well, so you were technically right! In common usage, though, I usually hear &quot;fluid&quot; for that meaning, while &quot;fluent&quot; usually relates, literally or metaphorically, to languages and comprehension.<br /> <br /> And yeah, 2e corebook reads super different from 1e corebook. 1e has a tone that struck me as a bit dry and verbose. 2e is colorful. (Sometimes a little too colorful, but usually a good balance.) There are also ideas that get emphasized or de-emphasized. For example, 1e talks more about mythologies of Longinus or Dracula as exceptional spontaneous Embraces, while 2e seems to imply that, while still rare, that might have happened more than modern vampires think. And 1e talks a lot about the World of Darkness as a stylistically exaggerated world, with higher crime rates and lots of gothic architecture, which I think is something the nWoD has moved away from over the years.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-10T20:45:40-07:00","id":586741,"title":"","content":"You can get a PDF copy of Reap the Whirlwind from drivethrurpg.com<br /> It is an introductory adventure with some of the 2e rules updates designed to let people test the game out. If you have a 1e corebook then you can compare the two and see which you like best. The setting changes are nuanced, so coming from Masquerade, the setting differences between editions aren't going to be as significant as the difference between Masquerade and Requiem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}